You want housing? Cool. We can help with that too. Except housing, it turned out, was tricky. It was also what Alan and just about everyone else wanted. So, okay, people want housing. So there were any number of housing providers open for business at that time. The system for getting into housing at that time in the 1980s and 90s was designed as a sort of staircase.
你想要住房？酷。我们也能帮到忙。除了住房，事实证明，这很棘手。这也是艾伦和几乎其他所有人想要的。所以，好吧，人们想要住房。因此，当时有任意数量的住房提供商在营业。在80和90年代，进入住房系统的过程被设计成了一种类似阶梯的结构。

18
00:08:39.000 --> 00:09:19.000
You started at the bottom in shelter and then if you fulfilled certain requirements like getting sober or taking your meds, you graduated to something sort of like a dormitory. From there, you might go to a shared apartment with a few other people. The final, final step at the very top of the staircase might be your own apartment in a building with other formerly homeless people, but it was hard to get there. The idea behind the staircase was something people called housing readiness. A kind of a quarantining to get people ready for housing and get them to get their life together so that they can get back into housing.
你从底层的收容所开始，然后如果你满足了某些要求，比如戒酒或按时服药，你就可以晋级到有点像宿舍的地方。从那里开始，你可能会去和其他几个人合租一套公寓。阶梯最顶端的最后一步，可能是你在一个住着其他曾经无家可归之人的大楼里拥有自己的公寓，但要到达那一步很难。阶梯背后的理念是人们所称的“住房准备度”。这是一种隔离期，为了让人们为住房做好准备，让他们把生活整顿好，以便能够重新回到住房中。

